.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt PAZARID 1
.Os pazari
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazarid
.Nd host memory daemon for pazari
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar path
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar path
.Cm example-config
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
runs on a virtualisation host and answers the guests that ask it for memory.
It listens for
.Xr pazari-agent 1
connections, decides whether a guest may grow, and actuates the decision by
resizing that guest's virtio-mem device over the hypervisor's QMP socket.
When the host itself comes under memory pressure, it asks a guest to hand
memory back.
.Pp
The daemon holds no durable state.
Every guest it knows about comes from its configuration file, and everything
else it knows it re-derives from
.Pa /proc
and from the hypervisor.
A restart loses nothing.
The guests reconnect, declare again what they hold, and carry on.
.Pp
With no subcommand the daemon runs in the foreground until it is asked to stop.
It reads its configuration once, at startup; there is no reload.
.Ss What it decides
A grow is granted when what the host has, less everything already promised to
other guests and less what this grow would take, still leaves
.Cm policy.host_floor .
What "what the host has" counts is
.Va MemAvailable ,
or
.Va MemAvailable
plus
.Va SwapFree
under
.Cm policy.grant_swap .
A grant is clamped to the guest's ceiling and may be for less than was asked.
.Pp
A refusal names one of four reasons, which the guest sees and the
.Va pazarid_denies_total
counter breaks out:
.Bl -tag -width "host_pressure" -offset indent
.It Cm ceiling
The request buys the guest nothing against its ceiling.
No retry advice: asking again will not help until the ceiling moves.
.It Cm host_pressure
The host does not have the memory right now, or has not yet read the figures it
would need to decide.
Worth retrying, and the daemon says when.
.It Cm rate_limited
The guest was granted memory less than
.Cm policy.grant_min_interval
ago.
The daemon names the time remaining.
.It Cm policy
The daemon does not know this guest, or its memory device has not yet reported
a size.
.El
.Pp
An offer to release is never refused for asking too often, and never refused
for want of host memory.
.Pp
A guest is never compelled to hand memory back.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c Ar path , Fl -config Ar path
The configuration file.
The default is
.Pa /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml .
Accepted before or after a subcommand.
.It Fl -version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm example-config
Print an annotated configuration file, carrying every key at its default, to
standard output.
.El
.Sh STARTUP
The daemon reads
.Pa /proc/meminfo
and fails to start if it cannot.
It then warns once if the guests at their ceilings plus
.Cm policy.host_floor
come to more than the host holds in RAM and swap together, refuses to start if
it was asked to revoke on a kernel that carries no
.Pa /proc/pressure/memory ,
and warns about zram swap it has been told to grant out of.
.Pp
It then binds the metrics listener, attaches to each guest's memory device,
binds one listener per distinct vsock port and one per unix socket, and tells
systemd it is ready.
.Pp
A guest whose QMP socket is not there yet is retried with a doubling backoff
rather than being treated as a configuration error, so the daemon and the VMs
it serves may be started in any order.
.Sh PRIVILEGE
The daemon needs no capabilities.
It reads
.Pa /proc ,
connects to QMP sockets, and binds its listeners; nothing it does requires
root, unless a guest is configured on a vsock port at or below 1023, which is
reserved.
.Pp
It does need to reach the hypervisor's QMP sockets, which is a matter of group
membership: run it as its own user and put that user in the group the sockets
belong to.
.Pp
A QMP socket carries the hypervisor's whole control interface, and anything
that can talk to it can do far more than resize a memory device.
Give the daemon a socket of its own so that its access can be reasoned about
separately from the VM manager's; see
.Xr pazari 7 .
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev RUST_LOG
The log filter, in the directive syntax the
.Va tracing
crate's environment filter reads.
The default is
.Cm info .
.It Ev JOURNAL_STREAM
Set by systemd.
Where it names the daemon's own standard error, records go to the journal at
their own priority, their fields under an
.Cm F_
prefix; otherwise they go to standard error.
.It Ev NOTIFY_SOCKET
Where the daemon tells systemd it is ready, and where it sends watchdog pings.
Set by systemd under
.Va Type=notify ;
ignored when it is not set.
.It Ev WATCHDOG_USEC
The watchdog period systemd expects to be pinged within.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
The default configuration file.
See
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 .
.It Pa /proc/meminfo
Read every tick for
.Va MemTotal , MemAvailable , SwapTotal
and
.Va SwapFree .
.It Pa /proc/pressure/memory
Read every tick for
.Va some avg10 .
Its absence is tolerated unless the daemon was asked to revoke.
.It Pa /proc/swaps
Read once at startup, under
.Cm policy.grant_swap ,
to look for zram.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Pp
The daemon exits non-zero when its configuration will not load, when
.Pa /proc/meminfo
cannot be read, and when it was asked to revoke on a kernel that cannot measure
memory pressure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Write a configuration file to start from:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pazarid example-config > /etc/pazari/pazarid.toml
.Ed
.Pp
Watch what it is doing, without a Prometheus:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9650/ | grep -E 'guest_size|host_available|denies'
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazari-stress 1 ,
.Xr pazari-agent.toml 5 ,
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 ,
.Xr pazari 7
.Pp
.Xr qemu 1 ,
.Xr proc 5
